    "# Question: Identify the two SQL window functions you would use — one for ranking and one for\n",
    "# retrieving the previous row's value — and explain why using window functions with PARTITION BY\n",
    "# produces more accurate and maintainable results for this requirement than a GROUP BY with a\n",
    "# self-join or correlated subquery approach.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Answer: SQL Window Functions\n",
    "\n",
    "We can use two SQL window functions:\n",
    "\n",
    "**1. ROW_NUMBER() / RANK() – For Ranking**\n",
    "\n",
    "RANK() is used to assign a rank to each row based on a specific order, such as highest sales or order amount.\n",
    "\n",
    "**2. LAG() – For Previous Row Value**\n",
    "\n",
    "LAG() is used to retrieve the value from the previous row without using a self-join.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Example SQL Query\n",
    "\n",
    "```sql\n",
    "SELECT\n",
    "    restaurant_id,\n",
    "    order_date,\n",
    "    total_orders,\n",
    "\n",
    "    RANK() OVER(\n",
    "        PARTITION BY restaurant_id\n",
    "        ORDER BY total_orders DESC\n",
    "    ) AS ranking,\n",
    "\n",
    "    LAG(total_orders) OVER(\n",
    "        PARTITION BY restaurant_id\n",
    "        ORDER BY order_date\n",
    "    ) AS previous_orders\n",
    "\n",
    "FROM restaurant_data;\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why use PARTITION BY?\n",
    "\n",
    "PARTITION BY divides the data into separate groups, such as individual restaurants.\n",
    "\n",
    "It calculates ranking and previous row values separately for each restaurant.\n",
    "\n",
    "Window functions make queries easier to read, maintain, and understand. They also avoid complicated self-joins and correlated subqueries.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Conclusion\n",
    "\n",
    "RANK() is used for ranking, and LAG() is used to retrieve the previous row's value. PARTITION BY ensures that calculations are performed separately within each group."
   ]
