    "# Question: Compare using a CTE (WITH clause) versus a nested subquery for this two-step\n",
    "# requirement. Evaluate the trade-offs in terms of readability, ability to reuse the intermediate\n",
    "# result, and debuggability. In what specific situation would you prefer the nested subquery over a\n",
    "# CTE?\n",
    "\n",
    "### Answer: CTE vs Nested Subquery\n",
    "\n",
    "**1. CTE (WITH Clause)**\n",
    "\n",
    "A CTE is used to create a temporary result set using the WITH clause.\n",
    "\n",
    "- It makes SQL queries easier to read and understand.\n",
    "- It allows us to reuse the intermediate result multiple times in the same query.\n",
    "- It is easier to debug because we can check each step separately.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Example:**\n",
    "\n",
    "```sql\n",
    "WITH order_summary AS (\n",
    "    SELECT restaurant_id, COUNT(*) AS total_orders\n",
    "    FROM orders\n",
    "    GROUP BY restaurant_id\n",
    ")\n",
    "SELECT * FROM order_summary;\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "**2. Nested Subquery**\n",
    "\n",
    "A nested subquery is a query written inside another SQL query.\n",
    "\n",
    "- It is useful for simple calculations.\n",
    "- It can make complex queries harder to read.\n",
    "- Reusing the intermediate result is difficult.\n",
    "- Debugging can be more challenging.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Example:**\n",
    "\n",
    "```sql\n",
    "SELECT *\n",
    "FROM (\n",
    "    SELECT restaurant_id, COUNT(*) AS total_orders\n",
    "    FROM orders\n",
    "    GROUP BY restaurant_id\n",
    ") AS order_summary;\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "### Comparison\n",
    "\n",
    "| Feature | CTE | Nested Subquery |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| Readability | Easy | Can be complex |\n",
    "| Reusability | Better | Limited |\n",
    "| Debugging | Easier | Difficult |\n",
    "| Best for | Complex queries | Simple queries |\n",
    "\n",
    "### When would I prefer a Nested Subquery?\n",
    "\n",
    "I would prefer a nested subquery when the requirement involves a small, simple calculation that is used only once. In this situation, a nested subquery keeps the SQL query short and simple.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Conclusion\n",
    "\n",
    "CTEs are better for complex, multi-step queries because they improve readability, reusability, and debugging. Nested subqueries are suitable for small and simple operations.\n"
   ]
